  "text_md": "Remark 3. According to Remark 2, each Iij can be written as an interval Iij = [Lij,Uij] where Lij and Uij are the lower and upper bounds of the interval, respectively. Also, each Sij (if it is non-empty) is obtained as one of the forms of Sij = {Lij}, Sij = {Uij}, Sij = {Lij,Uij} or Sij = [Lij,Uij].\n\nDeﬁnition 4. For each j ∈ J , we deﬁne Ij = i∈I Iij. Also, let Sij′ = Sij ∩ Ij, ∀i ∈ I and ∀j ∈ J . Corollary 4. Let i ∈ I and j ∈ J . If Sij′ = ∅, then Sij′ is as one of the forms of Sij′ = {Lj}, Sij′ = {Uj}, Sij′ = {Lj,Uj} or Sij′ = [Lj,Uj], where Lj and Uj are the lower and upper bound of Ij = [Lj,Uj], respectively. Proof. From Deﬁnition 4 and Remark 3, we have\n\nIj =\n\nIij =\n\n[Lij,Uij] = max\n\n{Lij} ,min i∈I\n\n{Uij} = [Lj,Uj]\n\ni∈I\n\ni∈I\n\ni∈I\n\nHence, for each i ∈ I , if Lij ∈ Ij (Uij ∈ Ij), then we must have Lij = Lj (Uij = Uj). On the other hand, from Remark 3, a non-empty Sij is as one of the forms of Sij = {Lij}, Sij = {Uij}, Sij = {Lij,Uij} or Sij = [Lij,Uij]. So, if Sij = {Lij} and Sij′ = Sij ∩ Ij = ∅, then Lij ∈ Ij; that is, Lij = Lj and therefore Sij′ = Sij ∩ Ij = {Lj}. For the other cases, the results are attained by the similar argument.\n\n![](<2503.05874_pg8_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<2503.05874_pg8_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n![](<2503.05874_pg8_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\n![](<2503.05874_pg8_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\nExample 1. Consider Problem (1) includes Yager operator where\n\nz = cTx = x1 + 0.35x2 + 0.93x3 + 3.28x4 + 5.03x5 + 2.96x6 + x7 + 2.75x8 + 5.25x9 + 6.39x10\n\n\n\n\n\n0.25 0.32 0.41 0.19 0.70 0.13 0.44 0.37 0.28 0.50 0.80 0.73 0.64 0.79 0.80 0.22 0.80 0.56 0.10 0.28 0.11 0.20 0.12 0.13 0.05 0.25 0.40 0.25 0.20 0.18 0.10 0.23 0.25 0.15 0.12 0.05 0.02 0.01 0.15 0.15 0.45 0.35 0.70 0.50 0.41 0.27 0.39 0.48 0.17 0.39 0.60 0.70 0.25 0.38 0.63 0.58 0.46 0.47 0.85 0.33 0.01 0.02 0.15 0.09 0.12 0.10 0.15 0.15 0.04 0.25 0.75 0.64 0.32 0.29 0.39 0.61 0.57 0.34 1.00 0.46 0.22 0.20 0.35 0.23 0.30 0.18 0.29 0.25 0.35 0.10 0.41 0.25 0.50 0.20 0.56 0.60 0.59 0.60 0.47 0.31\n\nA+ =\n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n0.70 0.70 0.32 0.44 0.00 0.16 0.20 0.50 0.40 0.39 0.70 0.65 0.14 0.12 0.80 0.76 0.00 1.00 0.15 0.79 0.17 0.24 0.20 0.20 0.06 0.25 0.13 0.19 0.22 0.02 0.14 0.10 0.04 0.00 0.10 0.00 0.14 0.02 0.15 0.08 0.70 0.04 0.27 0.36 0.60 0.40 0.48 0.50 0.50 0.50 0.66 0.63 0.14 0.73 0.53 0.46 0.61 0.85 0.85 0.39 0.00 0.15 0.15 0.05 0.02 0.03 0.10 0.12 0.18 0.09 0.63 0.03 0.55 0.77 0.79 0.49 0.21 0.32 0.80 0.71 0.27 0.30 0.35 0.24 0.35 0.07 0.29 0.35 0.20 0.75 0.59 0.34 0.26 0.38 0.02 0.60 0.52 0.43 0.27 0.44\n\nA− =\n\n \n\n \n\nbT = [0.50,0.80,0.25,0.15,0.50,0.75,0.15,0.80,0.35,0.60]\n\nIn this example, I = {1,2,...,10}, J = {1,2,...,10} and ϕ is the Yager t-norm (that is continuous and nilpotent) with p = 2. So, according to Table A1 we have\n\nϕ(x,y) = TY2(x,y) = max 0,1 − (1 − x)2 + (1 − y)2 1/2\n\n8\n\n",
